We can calculate the total number of shaded circles given some <imath>n</imath>. There are <imath>(n-1)^2</imath> full circles on the inside, <imath>4(n-1)</imath> semicircles on the sides, and <imath>4</imath> quarter circles for the corners. 

Full circles are, of course, worth one circle. Semicircles are worth half a circle each, and quarter circles are worth <imath>\dfrac14</imath> of a circle. Thus, weighing our sum gives <imath>(n-1)^2+\dfrac{4(n-1)}2+\dfrac44=n^2-2n+1+2(n-1)+1=n^2-2n+2n+2-2=n^2.</imath> Thus, there is <imath>n^2\cdot\pi r^2</imath> worth of the shaded area for any <imath>n</imath>, and since the area of each circle is <imath>\pi r^2</imath> if <imath>r</imath> is the radius of each. 

We want the ratio of this shaded area to the entire to be <imath>\dfrac12</imath>. The area of the entire square is <imath>n^2</imath>, so dividing, we see that <imath>\dfrac{n^2\cdot\pi r^2}{n^2}=\pi r^2=\dfrac12</imath>. 

The rest is the same as solution <imath>1</imath>.



== Solution 8 (most simple, easy ,estimation) ==

First, let's test what happens if <imath>d=0.5</imath>. If <imath>d=0.5</imath>, the area of the circle around each lattice point is <imath>\pi(0.5)^2 = 0.25\pi</imath>. Since each lattice point corresponds to a <imath>1 \times 1</imath> square of area, the probability of the point falling within <imath>0.5</imath> units is exactly <imath>\dfrac{0.25\pi}{1} \approx 0.785</imath>. Because this strictly exceeds our target probability of <imath>\dfrac{1}{2}</imath>, we know that <imath>d < 0.5</imath>. This conveniently eliminates <imath>0.5, 0.6,</imath> and <imath>0.7</imath>, and proves that the circles centered at the lattice points do not overlap.

Since the circles do not overlap, we can simply find the combined favorable area of the circles (and partial circles) inside the <imath>2020 \times 2020</imath> square. 

We can break this down into three types of lattice points:
There are <imath>2019^2</imath> interior lattice points, each contributing a full circle of area <imath>\pi d^2</imath>.
There are <imath>4 \cdot 2019</imath> edge lattice points (excluding corners), each contributing a semicircle of area <imath>\dfrac{1}{2}\pi d^2</imath>.
There are <imath>4</imath> corner lattice points, each contributing a quarter-circle of area <imath>\dfrac{1}{4}\pi d^2</imath>.

Summing these gives the total favorable area:
<imath>2019^2\pi d^2 + 4(2019)\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\pi d^2\right) + 4\left(\dfrac{1}{4}\pi d^2\right)</imath>

Factoring out the <imath>\pi d^2</imath>, we get a perfect square:
<imath>= (2019^2 + 2(2019) + 1)\pi d^2</imath>
<imath>= (2019 + 1)^2\pi d^2</imath>
<imath>= 2020^2\pi d^2</imath>.

The total area of the bounding <imath>2020 \times 2020</imath> square is <imath>2020^2</imath>. Therefore, the probability is the ratio of the favorable area to the total area:
<imath>\dfrac{2020^2 \pi d^2}{2020^2} = \pi d^2</imath>.

We are given that the probability is <imath>\dfrac{1}{2}</imath>, so we equate and solve:
<imath>\pi d^2 = \dfrac{1}{2} \implies d^2 = \dfrac{1}{2\pi}</imath>.

Solving for <imath>d</imath> gives <imath>d = \sqrt{\dfrac{1}{2\pi}} \approx \sqrt{0.159}</imath>. Since <imath>0.4^2 = 0.16</imath>, we can clearly see that <imath>d \approx 0.4</imath>.

by chundoowan

